  "notes": "The deepest traditional baritone of the 2010s radio cohort — ballad-dominant, steel-forward. The register logic is stated: keys are \"set low for the baritone,\" the kick is \"short and dry (register-clearing for the low voice)\" and the bass is \"high-passed relative to the norm; simple roots, long notes,\" so the bass floor sits a fifth above the genre's while its ceiling stays under the chord voicing. Drums are \"cross-stick verses … the default and the snare arrives only at the chorus,\" with \"claps and 16th hats\" reserved for the uptempo cuts — hence the low kick density, the clap layer and a hat base weighted toward 16ths only at the top of the band. Harmony is \"I–V–vi–IV and I–IV–V; ballads use a vi–IV–I–V rotation.\" The counter carries the record because the entry says the steel does: \"pedal steel is the primary partner voice.\" Melody is \"narrow range, low tessitura, long sustained notes, no melisma; the vocal's weight is the hook\" — which is why the octave-jump probability sits at the floor and the articulation is legato. ⚠ Confidence is medium, matching the research; the per-track readings are [LOW-CONF]. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the clap offsets, the perc density and the counter ratio.",
